A||||5||Richard Parnell||Fuel pump running||||5||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||04-21-2001||04:43 PM||||I noticed now that my fual pump seems to take longer to shut off when I turn the engine off.  It runs for up to 30 seconds sometimes more.  Is this an indication of a problem?||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||04-21-2001||04:50 PM||||It could just be your fuel pump relay, but it could also be an indicator that he fuel pump needs replacing.<P>The relay is located in the trunk on the firewall by the air cleaner......there are 2 identical relays.  You can swap these.....if the problem goes away then you know it was just a bad relay.<P>Another idea is the oil pressure sending unit, although the symtoms ae usually hard starts.<P>ood luck.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-21-2001||07:48 PM||||that would not likely be the relay. the op switch and relay contacts are wired parallel. <P>Did you recently change the oil? Fresh oil can hold pressure longer than old oil. Adding STP or other thick additives could have this result.<P>As long as it stays to about 30 seconds or less it's probably not a problem. At worst you are due to change the OP sender.<P>GM actually has a TSB about this because it's common to many GM cars.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Archie||04-21-2001||08:49 PM||||Is this on your V-8 car?<P>I'm pretty sure that you said that the guy who installed your V-8 swap rewired the whole engine compartment.<P>These kinds of problems are one reason I recommend to modify the stock harness rather than re-doing everything.<P>The normal diagnostics that you would use on a Fiero harness wouldn't necessarily apply here, depending on what all was re-wired and how it was done.<P>If this is on your V-8 car and if it is carburated and if it's hard to start soon after a warm shut down, then I'd say you have a stuck float or your fuel pressure regulator is out of adjustment.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000004||Richard Parnell||04-23-2001||03:04 PM||||Thanks guys.  Yes I am running thicker oil 20-50 in my car as we have a little warmer temps here.  Hopefully that's the reason the fuel pump stays on longer.  I do have an irritating problem with gas flow when I turn to the left.  Immediately after the turn, the oil pressuere goes way down and the engine has a major hesitation, like there's no gas.  My buddy solved this by turning his carb on the manifold to face foreward.  I don't want to do this because I have to reconfigure my throttle hook up then.  Any suggestions?||||reg||11
Z||000005||Richard Parnell||04-23-2001||07:12 PM||||Thanks Achie Ogre,  Yes I do run thicker oil in my car 20-50.  Hopefully that's whats' keeping the fuel pump activated longer.  Ialso have a problem when I take a fairly good left hand turn.  The oil pressure drops way down momentarily and the engines kicks like there's no gas in the line.  My friend got around this with his V8 Fiero by mounting the carb facing forward.  I'd rather not do this as I'd have to reconfigure the throttle cabling , etc.  Any ideas about that Problem . I know on the oil pressure drop I'd have to get a special oil pan with baffles.||||reg||5
